; The project verdict aliases, once, at the top of the test tree.
;
; In your own project these are called `cover` and `mutate` — the names
; windtrap's manual, skill, and own root dune use. They are renamed
; `example-*` HERE for one reason: this example lives inside windtrap's
; tree, both of those aliases are recursive there, and same-named
; aliases in a subdirectory would silently widen the project aggregate
; every time someone ran one at the root.
;
; Each alias runs every suite under it and merges what the suites wrote:
;
;   dune build @example-cover --instrument-with ppx_windtrap.coverage
;   WINDTRAP_MUTATE=1 dune build @example-mutate --force --instrument-with ppx_windtrap.mutate
;
; Coverage accumulates as a side effect of any instrumented run. A
; mutation VERDICT only exists if a suite was asked to test its mutants,
; so the mutation command carries WINDTRAP_MUTATE=1, the backend flag
; because the suites must carry the mutants, and --force because a
; mutation run is not a cached artifact; a plain `dune build
; @example-mutate` without them rebuilds the executables uninstrumented,
; which stales every verdict, and the merge then refuses loudly. The
; merge exits 1 when a mutant survived every executable that reached it.
; (universe) is load-bearing in both: the .coverage and .mutants files
; are written by test executables at exit and are not declarable
; dependencies, so it makes the milliseconds-cheap merge re-run on every
; build.

(rule
 (alias example-cover)
 (deps
  (alias_rec runtest)
  (universe))
 (action
  (run %{bin:windtrap} coverage --min 80)))

(rule
 (alias example-mutate)
 (deps
  (alias_rec runtest)
  (universe))
 (action
  (run %{bin:windtrap} mutate)))
